description:
Most strings in a program are short: names, keys, words. Allocating heap memory for each is slow, so real string classes (MSVC's `std::string` included) keep short strings **inside the object** and only use the heap for long ones. This is the **small string optimisation**. Build one:

```cpp
class SmallString
{
public:
    static constexpr std::size_t INLINE_CAPACITY{ 15 };

    SmallString();
    SmallString(const char* text);
    ~SmallString();
    SmallString(const SmallString& other);
    SmallString(SmallString&& other) noexcept;
    SmallString& operator=(SmallString other) noexcept;
    friend void swap(SmallString& a, SmallString& b) noexcept;

    void append(std::string_view more);
    std::size_t size() const;
    std::size_t capacity() const;
    bool isInline() const;
    const char* c_str() const;
    std::string_view view() const;
    bool operator==(std::string_view other) const;
};
```

- A string of up to 15 characters is stored in a 16-byte array **inside the object** (15 characters and the zero). `isInline()` is true and `c_str()` points inside the object itself.
- A longer string is stored on the heap with `new char[]`. `capacity()` is how many characters fit without allocating again (for an inline string, 15).
- **append** adds characters. When they no longer fit, it moves the text to a new heap block with capacity at least `max(2 * capacity, new size)`, copies the text over and frees any old heap block.
- `c_str()` is always zero-terminated.
- Copying makes an independent copy (inline when it fits); moving a heap string steals its block and leaves the source an empty inline string; moving an inline string copies its characters.
- Every heap block is freed exactly once.

